LOCALS(1)             KERNEL ARGUMENT BINDING FUNCTIONS                       LOCALS(1)NAME       locals - kernel argument binding and local variable functionsSYNOPSIS       locals ( -- )       (link) ( arguments -- frame) compilation       (unlink) ( frame return -- return) compilation       (unlink;) ( frame return -- return) compilation       (unlink>) ( frame return -- return) compilation              (local) ( -- addr) compilation       (local@) ( -- value) compilation       (local!) ( value -- ) compilation       -> ( x -- ) compilation       exit ( -- ) compilation       { ( -- ) immediate compilationDESCRIPTION       Kernel support for argument binding and local variables in       colon definitions. The virtual machine model support building       and disposing of argument frames.locals ( -- )       Vocabulary containing the argument binding and local variable       extension.(link) ( arguments -- frame) compilation       Compiled by "{" to perform the run-time action of building       the argument and local variable frame. These are built on the       parameter stack to minimize data movement. The frame consists       of the arguments and stack area for the local variables. The       old frame pointer and the top of stack are saved on the return       stack so that the stack and the frame pointer may be restored       after a definition using this extension.(unlink) ( frame return -- return) compilation       Drops the frame and move the return values down. The old       frame pointer and the top of stack at entry of the definition       are used to restore. (unlink;) ( frame return -- return) compilation       Performs the same action as "(unlink)" but also leaves the        definition. Is compiled by ";".(unlink>) ( frame return -- return) compilation       Performs the same action as "(unlink;)" but also performs       "(does>)". Is compiled by "does>".(local) ( -- addr) compilation       Using an inline literal offset within the current argument frame       returns the address of an argument or local variable.(local@) ( -- value) compilation       Using an inline literal offset within the current argument frame       access the value of an argument or local variable.(local!) ( value -- ) compilation       Using an inline literal offset within the current argument frame       store "value" to an argument or local variable. -> ( x -- ) compilation       Assigns an argument or a local variable. Used in the       following manner:               <value> -> <frame-variable>       exit ( -- ) compilation       Performs the equivalent action of the normal "exit" but also       restores the stack after argument binding and local variables.{ ( -- ) immediate compilation       Starts the named argument and local variable compiler. Used       in the following way:               { <argument-names> | <local-names> -- <any> }       or                { <argument-names> | <local-names> }       or               { <argument-names> -- <any> }       or               { <argument-names> }       or               { | <local-names> -- <any> }       or               { | <local-names> }       The different parts are optional. The only restriction is       that the definition is ended by "}" or "--". In the "--" case       characters until "}" are skipped. SEE ALSO       forth(1)WARNING       Code written using this word set is not directly portable to       other forth environments.BUG       Pointers are not check. Illegal pointer value will result in       a possible error situation.     COPYING       Copyright (C) 1989 Mikael R.K. Patel       Permission is granted to make and distribute verbatim copies       of this manual provided the copyright notice and this permission       notice are preserved on all copies.       Permission is granted to copy and distribute modified versions       of this manual under the conditions for verbat